+## 3. NEW dimension C — deploy-mirror build skew after #1782: primary Pages still runs the pre-fix SW; Vercel runs the fix (verified live)
+
+#1782 ("SW runtime asset caching was a live no-op… new swAssetManifestPlugin emits dist/sw-assets.json… sw.js loads it at install as the PRIMARY runtime-cache allowlist") landed in main ~06:00+08. QA II's mirror-parity check (earlier the same night) concluded "no parity drift at the funnel level". Post-#1782 deploy state, probed fresh:
+
+| Probe | pages.dev (primary) | vercel.app (mirror) |
+|---|---|---|
+| `sw.js` CACHE_NAME | **`supremeai-pwa-cache-v4`** (pre-fix) | **`supremeai-pwa-cache-v5`** (fix) |
+| Hashed-asset pattern in sw.js | `/[a-f0-9]{8,}/i` lowercase-hex-only — **the exact no-op #1782 fixed** | `/-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.[A-Za-z0-9]+$/` — corrected shape |
+| `sw-assets.json` | **`text/html` 6364 B — SPA-fallback illusion** (file absent; the 200 from an earlier curl is the index.html fallback) | `application/json` 1852 B, `generatedAt 2026-10-27T22:57:18.138Z`, real asset manifest |
+| App bundle set in index.html | `index-CAV1C5sl.js` + vendor-react/flow/charts | `index-C2hY5R9W.js` + vendor-react/events/ui — **different builds** |
+| Runtime cache after warm navigations (cache-storage probe) | `supremeai-pwa-cache-v4`: **0 entries** — runtime asset caching still the live no-op on the primary | `supremeai-pwa-cache-v5`: **6 asset entries** — **the #1782 manifest-allowlist fix verified working in production** |
+| Warm FCP (reload, SW controlled) | **156 ms** (TTFB 29 ms, doc transfer 6664 B) | **112 ms** (TTFB 16 ms, doc transfer 6502 B) |
+
+### F-C · HIGH (new, ops/deploy class) — primary deploy is stale: fix #1782 is live on the mirror only
+
+The primary host (`supremeai-studio.pages.dev`) serves a **pre-#1782 build** — old SW v4 with the broken pattern, empty runtime cache, stale bundle set — while the mirror (`vercel.app`) carries the fix and demonstrates it working (6 cached assets). Consequences: (a) primary-host repeat-visit users still have **zero offline asset resilience** (the exact defect #1782 closed stays live where most users are); (b) QA II's "no parity drift" verdict is **stale on this axis as of this cycle**; (c) the pair is a clean discriminating setup for whoever owns the Pages deploy: re-deploy Pages to main and the same cache probe should read v5 with non-zero assets. Warm-FCP numbers above corroborate the <1.0 s mandate on the warm path for the first time in the series (both hosts, both far under bar) — the skew is a resilience/ops issue, not a speed regression.
